====Verb====
{{en-verb|quits|quitting|quit|past2=quitted}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[leave]] (a place).
#:{{usex|en|The British '''quit''' India in the 1940s.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones
|passage=Jones had no sooner '''quitted''' the room, than the petty-fogger, in a whispering tone, asked Mrs Whitefield, “If she knew who that fine spark was?”}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1865|title=Proceedings of the Royal Geographical Society|page=33
|passage=He '''quitted''' the lake on the 23rd of September, and on the 4th of October arrived at Queenstown, on the shores of Lake Wakatipu, having succeded in finding a transitable route.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1912|text=The lions, if they left not the forest, would capture no prey; and the arrow, if it '''quitted''' not the bow, would not strike the mark.|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/34206/34206-h/34206-h.htm|translator=Edward Stanley Poole|title=The Thousand and One Nights}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1943|month=January and February|author=Chas. S. Lake|title=Some C.M.Es. I Have Known: IV—H. A. Ivatt|journal=Railway Magazine|page=32|text=At Malta the chief engineer of the ship, who always had been a good friend of mine, urged me to '''quit''' the sea; "otherwise," he said, "if you stay too long, you may, like myself, be condemned to wander about the world all your life and see your home only occasionally."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To set at rest; to free, as from anything harmful or oppressive; to relieve; to clear; to liberate.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1688|author=w:William Wake|title=Preparation for Death
|passage=To '''quit''' you {{...}} of this fear, {{...}}you have already lookt Death in the face; what have you found so terrible in it?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To release from obligation, accusation, penalty, etc.; to absolve; to acquit.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|book=Samson Agonistes|36|line=510|passage=God will relent, and '''quit''' thee all his debt;}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[abandon]], [[renounce]] (a thing).
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[resign]] from (a job, office, position, etc.).
#: {{ux|en|After having to work overtime without being paid, I '''quit''' my job.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[stop]], [[give up]] (an activity). {{+obj|en|gerund<q:usually>|verbal noun}}
#: {{ux|en|John is planning to '''quit''' smoking.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To [[close]] (an [[application]]).
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[pay]] (a debt, fine etc.).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1605|author=w:William Shakespeare
|passage=''Edmund'', enkindle all the sparkes of Nature<br>To quit this horrid acte.}}
#* {{RQ:Tasso Fairfax Godfrey of Bulloigne
|passage=that judge that '''quits''' each soul his hire}}
# {{lb|en|reflexive|archaic}} To conduct or [[acquit]] (oneself); to [[behave]] (in a specified way).
#* {{RQ:King James Version|1 Samuel|4|9|passage=Be strong and '''quit''' yourselves like men, O ye Philistines, that ye be not servants unto the Hebrews, as they have been to you: '''quit''' yourselves like men, and fight.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|book=Samson Agonistes|98|lines=1710–1711|''Samſon'' hath '''quit''' himſelf <br> Like ''Samſon'',}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To carry through; to go through to the end.
#* {{quote-book|en|start_year=1595|year=1609|author=w:Samuel Daniel|title=Civil Wars|text=Never worthy prince a day did '''quit''' <br> With greater hazard and with more renown.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[repay]], [[pay back]] (a good deed, injury etc.).
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|III|v|passage=Vnthankfull wretch (said he) is this the meed, <br> With which her soueraigne mercy thou doest '''quight'''?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1613|author=w:John Marston; William Barksted|title=The Insatiate Countess|section=III.2
|passage=Forgive me, Rogero: 'tis my fate <br> To love thy friend and '''quit''' thy love with hate.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[repay]] (someone) for (something).
#* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|4.14|2|II|xiv|passage=I was but late att a Iustynge <br> and there I Iusted with a knyghte that is broder vnto kynge Pellam <br> and twyes smote I hym doune <br> & thenne he promysed to '''quyte''' me on my best frynde <br> and so he wounded my sone that can not be hole tyll I haue of that knyghtes blood}}
#* {{quote-book|en|start_year=1515|year=c. 1516|year_published=1568|author={{w|John Skelton (poet)|John Skelton}}|title=Againſt venemous tongues enpoyſoned with ſclaunder and falſe detractions &c.|text=But if that I knewe what his name hight,<br>For clatering of me I would him ſone '''quight''';<br>For his falſe lying, of that I ſpake never,<br>I could make him ſhortly repent him forever:{{...}}}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The usual past tense of ''quit'' is now ''quit'' in most senses, although dictionaries may allow ''quitted'' as an alternative. ''Quitted'' is most commonly used to mean "departed", e.g., "Caesar '''quitted''' the neighborhood of Rome, and made for Campania with three legions."

